session_dirs_errors — retained startup validation
================================================

status: in_progress

Keep existing fail-fast startup validation for duplicate sessions and bad -d
roots. Diagnose both source locations and distinguish user-directory from
registered-component collisions. Do not silently drop a directory or start a
degraded server. Keep the parser/diagnostic and process-exit tests.

A1 Root entries can be inspected for duplicates before constructing the complete session structure.

T1 [scala-unit] fixture with two colliding roots; assert check() returns a Collision naming both root paths and lines, and does not throw.

A2 Session entry positions identify the ROOT file and line for diagnostics.

T2 [scala-unit] assert both are populated for a parsed Session_Entry.

A3 Comparing user directory roots with registered roots distinguishes their provenance.

T3 [scala-unit] a -d root colliding with a REGISTERED component session is classified From_Components, not From_Dir, and the message says "rename yours" rather than dropping the component. (The collision itself was reproduced by hand 2026-08-07 against the registered AFP: a user session named Alpha_Beta_Pruning. The shipped test uses "HOL" instead, deliberately — HOL is always in the baseline via the distribution's own ROOTS, unlike an AFP session name, so the test does not depend on AFP being registered in whatever environment runs it.) T3b [scala unit, added on review]: a ROOT file that EXISTS (so it is not a Bad_Dir) but fails to parse produces a Root_Error naming that ROOT path, exercising the second Exn.capture layer (forcing Root_File.entries) and the decode_message path Bad_Dir/Root_Error details travel. Not covered by T1/T2/T3, which only exercise Collision and Bad_Dir; this was a gap until review caught it.

A6 [heap] The pre-scan runs only when custom directories are supplied; retain bounded startup behavior.
